Z||000000||Greg Piet||04-12-2000||04:41 PM||||1985 2.8 v6<BR>replaced the rings, and smoke is gone.<BR>Car ran ok for 5 miles then died!<BR>Apparently it is flooding.<BR>I have spark, i have compression.<BR>without cranking I get 12 v from the injector harness for the normal injectors, but 0 v from the cold start  injector wires.   Is this normal.<BR>I have resistance of about 4 ohms from the injectors and 6 ohms from the cold start.<P>What could it be?<BR>My only ideas ore a stuck injector.<P>PS:<BR>coolant sensor works (and the gauge is accurate)   the TPS has 5v at its leads except for the ground.<BR>the TPS resistance is in spec.<P><BR>Also there is a 2 wire sensor on the passenger side of the block closer to the back of the car than the EGR solenoid...  what is it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-13-2000||03:30 AM||||If I remember correctly. The fuel regulator does this funny thing when the vacuum hose is not on correctly or at all. It allows the injectors to flow full blast! Other than this I would be just as mystified as you are. I hope thats all it is. Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||04-13-2000||03:13 PM||||Ok first thing is that im a big idiot!<BR>I didnt realize this but that mystery sensor i asked about in this post was unplugged and it is the Temp sensor!<BR>I thought the cold start sensor was the temp sensor.  When I did the troubleshooting I thought the wires that were actually for the cold  start switch were the coolant temp wires.  I then assumed those wires were bad and i wired it up unknowingly so that the ECM got its coolant temp info from the cold start sensor.  This allowed the car to run for about 5 miles then it flooded!  I realized my stupidity and fixed it all up.<BR>So...   smoke is gone and it passed emissions w/o any problems!  YIPPEE!<P>But now my temp gauge doesnt work, and as the car warms up the coolant temp idiot light brightens as the car gets hotter.  Im assuming i fried the ground wire in the gauge itself so i have to rip it apart.  The ECM seems to be getting proper info from the coolant temp sensor, thus im thinking the problem is in the gauge itself.<P>OK now you can all laugh at my stupidity!    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||04-13-2000||04:48 PM||||Idiot light changing brightness would lead me to believe that your plug to the gage sender/idiot light sensor has melted and the wires are plugged directly into the sensor. If this is so sounds like they are reversed. This is the sensor located by in the head down by the distrib. Just an idea.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||04-13-2000||08:04 PM||||Well that fixed the problem!!!   Thanks!<BR>That sensor is called the Gauge sender?<BR>I was curious so I could dive into the schematics of that sensor.   That way I could answer the question "Does that sensor produce error codes or what?"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||04-13-2000||10:38 PM||||The sender for the temp gage and idiot light has no input to the computer. The idiot light part is closed above 250F and open below 230F<BR>The gage part is 1365 ohms@100F and 55 ohms@260F If you reverse the two wires at both the sender and the dash your gage won't peg and the light will light on startup||||reg||1
